## 3. Consequential issue→fix→regression-test chain (verified, with applicability limits)

Found via live GitHub search this stage (capture sha256 `7e5a310f45ef65ece39acbd9f8a7cf0b2ed33d980b1167b3a05a06ebe8c6626b`, search "repo:pandas-dev/pandas on_bad_lines type:pr", 27 hits). Chain, most recent last:

**C-a (historical root).** pandas issue #5686 (referenced by PR #45146, "ENH: Allow callable for on_bad_lines in read_csv when engine=\"python\"", merged 2022-01-08, milestone 1.4, "closes #5686", tests + whatsnew checked). Establishes that bad-line handling was being extended engine-by-engine — the same API behaves differently across `engine="c"` / `engine="python"`, a core data-contract hazard.

**C-b (silent-skip hazard, released).** PR #50311 "DOC: Add to the io documentation of `on_bad_lines` to alert users of **silently skipped lines**", merged 2023-01-03, milestone "2.0" (milestone object shows `closed_at 2023-04-03T21:02:30Z`, i.e., the 2.0 release timestamp — indirect release evidence). Body: with a user-defined callable, "any bad line that isn't a 'line with too many fields' is **silently skipped**"; closes discussion issue #48020. Directly supports the brief's prohibition on silently deleting malformed records: even the safe-looking callable path silently drops rows unless the callable re-inserts them.

**C-c (bad-line handling silently disabled; behavioral fix on main).** PR #68946 "BUG: python-engine read_csv ignored on_bad_lines with index_col=False (GH#49279)", merged 2026-09-17, **no milestone** (so: in `main`; release applicability unestablished; pandas 2.2.3 per S1 predates it and retains the buggy behavior). PR body: with `index_col=False` the python parser "skipped bad-line handling entirely, so `on_bad_lines=\"error\"` did not raise, `\"skip\"` did not skip, and a callable was never called; a row with more fields than expected was **truncated** instead"; condition dated to the original `index_col=False` commit (GH-2442); fix makes expected width `max(column count, first data row, header line)`; **deliberate default-behavior change** — ragged rows + `index_col=False` now raise `ParserError`; two existing tests (GH-46955, GH-46569) updated to the new behavior. Explicitly left open in the same body: `on_bad_lines="skip"` "does not refill `nrows`/`chunksize` after a dropped row" — i.e., on pandas `main` (as of capture), a chunked/bounded read of a dirty file yields short chunks after skips. Limits: PR-body self-report; merged ≠ released; I did not fetch the diff to enumerate the regression test files.

**C-d (streaming-path memory blow-up; fix + regression test, unreleased).** PR #70406 "BUG: read_csv chunked read with skipped bad lines ran out of memory", labels Bug + **Regression**, milestone **3.1** (milestone still open), merged 2026-10-05 (one day before this critique). Body: `pd.read_csv(path, chunksize=N, on_bad_lines="skip")` (C engine) raised `ParserError: out of memory` on ordinary files "e.g. 300k rows with 2% bad rows at chunksize=100"; root cause: the parser recorded its rounded-up word-buffer capacity as a high-water mark and reserved from it, so capacity "roughly doubled every couple of reads until allocation failed"; fix records the largest request instead, bounded by input size; the mark's original overflow protection (GH-23509) is covered by `end_line` padding (GH-66657); **version applicability**: reachable only since the GH-40587 fix (#66944) "new in 3.1.0"; "pandas 3.0.6 never skipped bad lines in chunked reads, so it does not hit this"; body asserts fix, test, and whatsnew were written (test identity not independently verified — see U2). Limits: PR-body evidence; 3.1.0 not released as of capture.

Why this chain matters to the brief: the proposed workspace's data contract (no silent drops, bounded preview, streaming path for ≥5 GB files) sits exactly on these three failure modes — silent skipping (C-b), silently disabled validation in a common configuration (C-c), and a memory blow-up on the skip+chunk path (C-d). Any proposal that says "use pandas chunked reads with on_bad_lines=skip for the preview" is, on today's evidence, recommending a path with a known unreleased OOM regression and a known short-chunk defect.

